.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS BOOLEAN
.IP
.EX
#define BOOLEAN
#define boolean
.EE
.PP
The portable boolean type.
.PP
It is \f[CR]bool\f[R] wherever the language has one, \f[CR]_Bool\f[R] on
C89 compilers that accept it as an extension, and
\f[CR]unsigned char\f[R] everywhere else.
.PP
Converting any nonzero value to \f[CR]BOOLEAN\f[R] gives
\f[CR]TRUE\f[R], except on the \f[CR]unsigned char\f[R] fallback, where
only the low 8 bits survive.
Write \f[CR](BOOLEAN)(VALUE != 0)\f[R] when that matters.
.PP
\f[CR]boolean\f[R] is the lowercase alias.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS TRUE
.IP
.EX
#define TRUE
#define true
.EE
.PP
The true value of the chosen type: \f[CR]true\f[R] wherever
\f[CR]bool\f[R] exists, \f[CR]1\f[R] everywhere else.
.PP
An earlier \f[CR]TRUE\f[R] (for example from \f[CR]<windows.h>\f[R]) is
replaced.
It always meant \f[CR]1\f[R], so nothing changes for code that used it.
.PP
\f[CR]true\f[R] is only defined when the language has none of its own.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS FALSE
.IP
.EX
#define FALSE
#define false
.EE
.PP
The false value of the chosen type: \f[CR]false\f[R] wherever
\f[CR]bool\f[R] exists, \f[CR]0\f[R] everywhere else.
.PP
An earlier \f[CR]FALSE\f[R] (for example from \f[CR]<windows.h>\f[R]) is
replaced.
It always meant \f[CR]0\f[R], so nothing changes for code that used it.
.PP
\f[CR]false\f[R] is only defined when the language has none of its own.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS bool
.IP
.EX
#define bool
.EE
.PP
Only defined when the language has no \f[CR]bool\f[R] of its own: C89
and older, and C++ compilers older than the keyword.
It names the same type as \f[CR]BOOLEAN\f[R], so code written for
\f[CR]<stdbool.h>\f[R] still compiles.
.PP
The guards of the known \f[CR]<stdbool.h>\f[R] headers are closed at the
same time, so a later \f[CR]#include <stdbool.h>\f[R] is a no\-op
instead of a clash.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS __bool_true_false_are_defined
.IP
.EX
#define __bool_true_false_are_defined
.EE
.PP
The standard macro that says \f[CR]bool\f[R], \f[CR]true\f[R] and
\f[CR]false\f[R] are ready to use, the same way \f[CR]<stdbool.h>\f[R]
defines it.
Always \f[CR]1\f[R].
